9front userland source, carried so that this tree can build it itself.

Revision
	a0a079f4054f88eabcb2c2e6d20f3ee4a1908b78
	from git.9front.org/plan9front/9front, every directory below taken
	at that one revision.  The include set in include/ is sys/include
	at the same revision; libc/{port,fmt,9sys,amd64} is sys/src/libc;
	the lib* directories are sys/src/lib*; cmd/rio is sys/src/cmd/rio.

	One header the build needs is not under this directory:
	libc/amd64/notejmp.c includes <ureg.h>, and the amd64 Ureg lives
	in 9front/amd64/include/ureg.h beside the u.h and lib9.h that were
	already there.  It was untracked until now, so a fresh clone could
	not build libc; it is tracked.  Nothing else the build includes
	resolves outside 9front/src/include and 9front/amd64/include -
	all twenty-five of them were checked.

Why it is here
	9front/amd64/lib holds prebuilt archives, and nothing in this tree
	had ever linked them.  The reason is that it cannot: those objects
	were made by 9front's own 6c, which emits instructions past the end
	of this linker's table, and 6l here reads 228 of the 262 members of
	libc.a and rejects 34 with "opcode out of range".  The compiler is
	not the problem - this tree's 6c compiles current 9front source
	with no warnings - so the answer is to build from source, and the
	source is what was missing.

What is not here
	libc/9syscall.  The system-call stubs are generated on a real Plan
	9 out of a sys.h and the kernel's own table, so there is no source
	file to build; they come from the prebuilt archive, which is linked
	after the source-built libc and answers only what the source
	cannot.  They are also every one of them readable by this linker.

	libc/amd64/main9.s and main9p.s, libc's own entry.  These exist at
	that revision; they are left out by choice, not by absence.  This tree's
	programs are entered by os/osprey/pc/init9*.c instead, because the
	kernel here enters a program with the argument vector in the first
	register rather than the Tos, and because that member is one of the
	34 this linker cannot read.

	Every architecture of libc but amd64.  libthread's directory was
	taken whole, 386.c and arm.c and the rest with it; the build
	script names the one file it compiles and skips those.

How it is built
	os/osprey/cmd/p9/build9front.sh	the ten libraries, to 9front/amd64/lib-src
	os/osprey/cmd/p9/build9bin.sh	rio and the draw, mouse and keyboard
					servers, to 9front/amd64/bin-src

	Both are generated directories and are not tracked.

Who links it
	lib-src/libc9.a is not off to the side.  The four legacy programs
	the pair personality boots - catp9, execp9, shp9 and legacyp9 -
	link it ahead of the prebuilt archive, in place of the four lib9
	modules they used to compile as a workaround, so the criteria of
	scripts/osprey-check-p9pair.py are what say this library runs and
	not merely links.  Of the 34 members the linker cannot read, those
	four programs require three - vfprint, dofmt and vsnprint - so
	what the criteria exercise is the formatting path.  pool, malloc
	and the Dir conversions build here and are not yet reached by
	anything that boots; they are what rio reaches.

Local changes
	None.  Every file below is as it is at that revision; anything this
	tree needs differently is done in os/osprey, not here.
